// Self-host Stage 2: BLOCK-LOCAL STORE-TO-LOAD FORWARDING for `field_set` /
// `field_get` (#2426). Split out of opt.bit the way optparams.bit was — same
// module, so nothing here needs an import.
//
// ## What it does
//
// `x.f = v` followed by a read of `x.f` kept both instructions, so
// `n = n + 1; return n` on a heap cell paid a store and an immediate reload.
// The captured-counter idiom is the visible case (#2128 made a captured
// variable a one-word heap cell), but the same shape is every multi-value
// return: lowering builds a tuple with `field_set` and destructures it with
// `field_get` three instructions later. This pass points the load at the
// stored value; DCE then drops the load.
//
// ## The invalidation rule, which IS the correctness argument
//
// A forward is legal only when nothing between the store and the load can have
// written that slot. Three rules, each of which refuses rather than reasons
// when it cannot prove disjointness.
//
// 1. TWO BASES MAY COEXIST ONLY WHEN THEY ARE TWO DISTINCT `gc_alloc`s.
//    Every other pair clears: a store through a base that is not an allocation
//    result drops every entry it does not itself own. So this pass still never
//    has to answer "can these two SSA values point at the same object" — the
//    question it would get wrong. Two distinct SSA values genuinely can alias:
//    `append(s, x)` hands back the caller's own backing array, a `bitcast`
//    renames a pointer, and any two references to one object are the same
//    memory.
//
//    The one fact it does use is not an alias analysis, it is the defining
//    property of the allocator: `gc_alloc` returns an object no other value
//    denotes, so two DIFFERENT `gc_alloc` instructions return two DIFFERENT
//    objects. Nothing derived — not a `bitcast` of an allocation, not a value
//    read back out of one — qualifies; the test is literally "is this value id
//    the result of a `gc_alloc` instruction" (`storeBaseIsFresh`).
//
//    #4413 filed this as "let StoreTable hold several bases, because a store
//    through base X cannot be ANSWERED by a load through base Y". That argument
//    is about the lookup side and it is not the one that matters: the hazard is
//    the KILL side. If X and Y alias, `field_set Y[0]` overwrote `X[0]`, and an
//    entry for `X[0]` that survives it forwards a stale value. Multi-base
//    without a distinctness fact is a miscompile, which is why there is one.
//
// 2. WITHIN ONE BASE, A DIFFERENT BYTE OFFSET IS A DIFFERENT SLOT. `ival`
//    is the byte offset `structLayout` assigned (compiler/loweraccess.bit:66),
//    and one SSA value has one static type, hence one layout, whose fields do
//    not overlap. So `field_set B[8] = v` leaves an entry for `B[0]` intact,
//    which is what lets the `(u8, i32, u8)` tuple in
//    _tests_/cases/ir_tuple_layout.bit forward all three of its reads.
//
// 3. ANYTHING ELSE THAT COULD WRITE MEMORY CLEARS THE TABLE, and that list is
//    NOT a new one. `isSideEffecting` (opt.bit) is already this compiler's
//    answer to "can this op trap, allocate, or write observable state" — DCE
//    deletes any instruction it does not name, so "writes memory => listed" is
//    an invariant the tree already stakes correctness on. Reusing it means an
//    op added later is a barrier here the day it lands rather than silently
//    transparent, and there is no second table to drift out of step with the
//    first. Exactly three members are excluded, each accounted for:
//    `field_set`, which rules 1 and 2 invalidate precisely; `index_get`, a LOAD
//    whose only effect is a bounds-check panic, and a panic does not return;
//    and `gc_alloc`, which invalidates partially — see below.
//
// `make_closure` is therefore a barrier. `gc_alloc` is the third exception and
// is a PARTIAL one: it drops every entry whose base is not an allocation this
// function made, and keeps the rest (`storeTableCollect`).
//
// ## Why a `gc_alloc` does not have to drop an entry based on an allocation
//
// NOT because the collector is non-moving. That is a runtime property
// (runtime/ABI.md:865), it is exactly the kind of thing a pass must not
// silently depend on, and this pass does not: the argument below holds for a
// relocating collector too.
//
// Take an entry `A[o] = v` where `A` is the result of a `gc_alloc` in this
// function, and a collection at a later `gc_alloc`. For the forwarded program
// to differ from the un-forwarded one, the collection would have to leave the
// slot `A[o]` denoting something other than what `v` denotes. It cannot:
//
//   - `A` is read after the collection, so it is live there, so it is a root in
//     the stack map and any relocating collector updates it with the object.
//   - a scalar field is copied verbatim by any collector, moving or not.
//   - a POINTER field is at a traced offset (`gc_alloc`'s own `ptrs=[...]`), so
//     a relocating collector rewrites it — and it rewrites `v` in lockstep,
//     because forwarding is what makes `v` live across that safepoint and the
//     stack map is built from the FINAL IR: `optimizeModule` runs to completion
//     in `buildObjCmd`/`writeModuleExe` before any backend sees the function,
//     and the maps come from `codegenlive.bit`'s liveness over that.
//   - a collector that updated one of the two and not the other would already
//     have broken the UN-forwarded program, whose later `field_get A[o]` and
//     whose other uses of `v` must agree.
//
// So forwarding across an allocation introduces no failure mode the program
// does not already have. What is dropped instead is every entry whose base this
// pass did not watch being allocated — a parameter, a field read, anything
// reached from outside — because for those the first bullet is not available
// and the blast radius is not worth the reload.
//
// `gc_alloc` stays in `isSideEffecting`, so DCE still never deletes one and
// optalloc.bit still owns the only removal path.
//
// ## What it deliberately does not do
//
// - No load-to-load forwarding (CSE of `field_get`). Sound under the same three
//   rules, but a different transform and not this ticket's.
// - Nothing across blocks. A predecessor's store is available only where it
//   dominates, which needs a dominator walk this pass does not have.

// The stores available for forwarding at the current point of one block. Entry
// `i` is "`base[i]` at byte offset `off[i]` holds SSA value `val[i]`". `n` is
// how many of the parallel arrays' slots are live: the arrays only grow, so a
// kill compacts into the prefix and allocates nothing.
class StoreTable {
  n: int,
  base: []int,
  off: []int,
  val: []int,
}

fn newStoreTable(): StoreTable {
  return StoreTable{ n: 0, base: []int(0), off: []int(0), val: []int(0) }
}

fn storeTableClear(t: StoreTable) {
  t.n = 0
}

// Writes entry `i`, appending when the arrays have not reached that slot yet.
// Only ever called with `i <= t.n`, so it appends at most once per call.
fn storeTableSet(t: StoreTable, i: int, base: int, off: int, val: int) {
  if (i < len(t.base)) {
    t.base[i] = base
    t.off[i] = off
    t.val[i] = val
    return
  }
  t.base = append(t.base, base)
  t.off = append(t.off, off)
  t.val = append(t.val, val)
}

// Rule 1's distinctness fact, and the whole of it: is this value id the direct
// result of a `gc_alloc`? Nothing derived from one counts — a `bitcast` of an
// allocation and a pointer read back out of a field are both other value ids
// with other defining ops, so both answer false and both therefore clear.
fn storeBaseIsFresh(f: IrFunc, base: int): bool {
  if (base < 0 || base >= len(f.instrs)) {
    return false
  }
  return f.instrs[base].op == Op.GcAlloc
}

// Whether a store of `base[off]` leaves the entry `other[otherOff]` intact.
// Same base: rule 2, a different byte offset is a different slot. Different
// base: rule 1, which needs the two to denote different objects and has exactly
// one way to know that.
fn storeKeepsEntry(f: IrFunc, base: int, off: int, other: int, otherOff: int): bool {
  if (other == base) {
    return otherOff != off
  }
  return storeBaseIsFresh(f, base) && storeBaseIsFresh(f, other)
}

// The value id last stored at `base[off]`, or -1 when this pass cannot prove
// one is still there.
fn storeTableFind(t: StoreTable, base: int, off: int): int {
  let i = 0
  while (i < t.n) {
    if (t.base[i] == base && t.off[i] == off) {
      return t.val[i]
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return -1
}

// Records `base[off] = val`, dropping exactly the entries rules 1 and 2 cannot
// keep. Bounded: every surviving entry was already in the table and one is
// added, so the table never exceeds the number of `field_set`s seen in the
// block, and a block has statically many.
fn storeTableStore(f: IrFunc, t: StoreTable, base: int, off: int, val: int) {
  let w = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < t.n) {
    if (storeKeepsEntry(f, base, off, t.base[i], t.off[i])) {
      storeTableSet(t, w, t.base[i], t.off[i], t.val[i])
      w = w + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  storeTableSet(t, w, base, off, val)
  t.n = w + 1
}

// A `gc_alloc` may collect. Keeps the entries whose base is an allocation this
// function made and drops every other — see the header for why that is not the
// non-moving-collector dependency it looks like.
fn storeTableCollect(f: IrFunc, t: StoreTable) {
  let w = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < t.n) {
    if (storeBaseIsFresh(f, t.base[i])) {
      storeTableSet(t, w, t.base[i], t.off[i], t.val[i])
      w = w + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  t.n = w
}

// Rule 3: the ops this pass refuses to look through. `isSideEffecting` minus
// the three members it can account for exactly — see the header. Deriving it
// rather than listing it is the point: a new op that writes memory must join
// `isSideEffecting` or DCE would already delete it, and joining it makes it a
// barrier here for free.
fn opClobbersFieldSlots(op: Op): bool {
  return isSideEffecting(op) && op != Op.FieldSet && op != Op.IndexGet && op != Op.GcAlloc
}

// Answers one `field_get` from the table when a preceding store proves the
// value. THE TYPE GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING, not a formality: codegen takes a
// store's width from its VALUE operand's type and a load's from its result type
// (compiler/arm64compile.bit:232, compiler/x64dispatch.bit:358), so forwarding
// an i64 store into an i32 read would silently widen what the program sees.
fn forwardOneLoad(f: IrFunc, t: StoreTable, fwd: []int, idx: int) {
  let ins = f.instrs[idx]
  let src = storeTableFind(t, ins.lhs, ins.ival)
  if (src < 0) {
    return
  }
  if (f.instrs[src].ty != ins.ty) {
    return
  }
  fwd[idx] = src
}

// One block, front to back. Block params are skipped: they read and write
// nothing, and `rebuild` emits them ahead of this range anyway.
fn forwardBlockStores(f: IrFunc, fwd: []int, bi: int) {
  let blk = f.blocks[bi]
  let t = newStoreTable()
  let idx = blk.instsStart + blk.paramCount
  let end = blk.instsStart + blk.instsLen
  while (idx < end) {
    let ins = f.instrs[idx]
    if (ins.op == Op.FieldGet) {
      forwardOneLoad(f, t, fwd, idx)
    } else if (ins.op == Op.FieldSet) {
      storeTableStore(f, t, ins.lhs, ins.ival, ins.rhs)
    } else if (ins.op == Op.GcAlloc) {
      storeTableCollect(f, t)
    } else if (opClobbersFieldSlots(ins.op)) {
      storeTableClear(t)
    }
    idx = idx + 1
  }
}

// `n` entries of -1: "forward nothing", for the rebuilds that do no forwarding
// (DCE, and the inliner's translate-and-copy).
fn noForwarding(n: int): []int {
  let fwd = []int(0)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    fwd = append(fwd, -1)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return fwd
}

// The forwarding map for `f`: `fwd[id]` is the value id that `id` is provably
// equal to, or -1 for "emit it". `rebuild` (optrebuild.bit) consults it and the DCE
// pass behind it removes the loads this made dead.
//
// Block-local, so no dominance question arises — a forwarded load and the store
// answering it sit in the same block, in that order, and `rebuild` walks blocks
// in ascending `instsStart`, so the source's `remap` entry is always already
// written when the load is reached.
fn forwardFieldLoads(f: IrFunc): []int {
  let fwd = noForwarding(len(f.instrs))
  let b = 0
  while (b < len(f.blocks)) {
    forwardBlockStores(f, fwd, b)
    b = b + 1
  }
  return fwd
}
